  "verified_against": {
    "source": "release-manifest.json",
    "agrees": true,
    "note": "Hashes were downloaded from the published v5.36.0 release assets and hashed independently, then checked again by re-fetching the URL this pin itself names. Only the pegasus artifact changed: install.sh and the two builder scripts are byte-identical to v5.28.0 and earlier. v5.36.0 does two things that matter here. It declares, where the map is read, that the runtime folds edit, write and apply_patch onto a single permission -- so the wildcard this render emits does not close what it does not name, and writing apply_patch into either table would be a key nothing reads rather than a restriction. That debt does not close: the tool still reaches every agent that can write, and the other way out depends on a third party that has declined it twice. What closes is the possibility of reading the render wrong from inside the product. And it makes the suite stop waiting on the disk: fsync measures 6302 microseconds on ext4 against 1.2 on tmpfs, a test that installed 113 artifacts paid 226 of those waits, and there is now one single way to create a throwaway home -- in memory, falling back to disk where there is no /dev/shm -- with a guard that requires it. This distribution changed nothing of its own for this pin beyond the alignment."
  }
